<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">2011 Christchurch earthquake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2011">2011</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> earthquake was a magnitude 6.2 earthquake that struck Christchurch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, on 22 February 2011. Centred close to the city, it killed 185 people, many in the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> Television building, and destroyed much of the central business district, including the spire of the cathedral. It was one of the deadliest disasters in New Zealand's history, and rebuilding took more than a decade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/2011-christchurch-earthquake.jpg" alt="2011 Christchurch earthquake" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greg O'Beirne · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ChristchurchBasilicaPostEarthquake_gobeirne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The earthquake struck at 12:51 p.m. local time on Tuesday 22 February 2011, centred 6.7 kilometres (4.2 mi) south-east of the central business district. Scientists classified it as an intraplate earthquake. GNS Science considers it part of the aftershock sequence of the magnitude 7.1 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> earthquake of 4 September 2010, although a seismologist from Geoscience Australia treats it as a separate event because it lay on a separate fault system. Both quakes occurred on blind or unknown faults, though a 1991 report by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s Earthquake Commission had predicted moderate earthquakes in Canterbury, with liquefaction. Buildings and infrastructure were already weakened by the 2010 quake and its aftershocks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The shaking lasted about 10 seconds, but the damage was severe because the focus was shallow and close to the city. Peak ground accelerations were the highest ever recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, and the movement was mainly vertical, with eyewitnesses describing people being tossed into the air. Liquefaction in the eastern suburbs produced around 400,000 tonnes of silt. Many older buildings, especially those of unreinforced masonry or built before stringent codes, were damaged. Because the quake came at lunchtime, some people on the footpaths were buried by collapsed buildings. It killed 185 people from more than 20 countries, and over half of the deaths were in the six-storey Canterbury Television building, which collapsed and caught fire.</p><p>Eighty per cent of the city lost power at first. A local state of emergency was superseded on 23 February by a national one, only the second in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s history, which remained in force until 30 April. Search and rescue teams from New Zealand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, the United Kingdom, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> worked in the city, and the New Zealand Defence Force carried out its largest operation on New Zealand soil. A Central City Red Zone was set up on the day of the quake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The government set up a Royal Commission in March 2011 to examine the causes of building failure and the adequacy of building codes, and the Canterbury Earthquake Recovery Authority was created on 29 March. About 10,000 houses were expected to need demolition and over 100,000 were damaged. Areas ruined by liquefaction and rockslides were placed in residential red zones, and through voluntary buyouts the Crown and insurers removed over 8,000 homes; the last demolition was completed in October 2021.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> could not host its Rugby World Cup matches in 2011, and the national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> planned for 8 March was postponed to 2013. In the year to June 2011 the city's population fell by 8,900, and it took until 2017 to recover the loss. A national memorial service was held on 18 March at North Hagley Park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “2011 Christchurch earthquake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The country has also faced severe shocks: the <b>Christchurch earthquake</b> of 22 February 2011 killed 185 people, and on 15 March 2019 a gunman killed 51 worshippers at two Christchurch mosques, after which most semi-automatic weapons were banned within a month.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
